/** Editorial translations for siman 187 failing blocks — roeh machmat tashmish / wound / birth */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `Three times. Meaning with the first — that is, still a second time; and Shach wrote: but after this measure she is pure even though we say she saw earlier — she is removed from the category of seeing blood due to relations; but if she examined immediately after relations and found nothing, and afterward saw even within this measure — it is not called seeing blood due to relations. And per this, for us who hold that all near relations is called seeing blood due to relations, as the Rabbi wrote — this is specifically when it is known with certainty that she saw near relations; but if long after relations she examined herself and found blood, even though we say she saw at time of relations — she is permitted; and all the more a woman who saw three times in the morning on the night of her immersion — when she rose from her bed she found on her garment an abundance of blood — she is permitted. Again the Gaon A.M.U. z"l ruled that even if this woman has relations again once or twice and finds immediately at time of relations — she is permitted; and we do not say the matter was revealed retroactively that it was also at time of relations — rather the first days fall away and from here onward we calculate. And the matter is simple: wherever it is not called seeing blood due to relations, even if she saw many many times — she is permitted; and so too in responsum Maharam of Lublin siman 102 — there is no distinction whether she had relations three times or forty and fifty times — end quote.`,
    '1#ה': `Experts. Shach wrote, and it is questionable whether we are stringent from this reason that we are not experts to forbid her to everyone forever where she was established with three men — end quote.`,
    '10#ב': `Consecutive. Shach wrote — meaning if it happened that she saw immediately after her birth three consecutive times, and afterward passed and had relations in between and did not see, and afterward returned and saw after her birth three consecutive times and did not have relations again — she is permitted; and likewise even if she saw after each birth three consecutive times, only afterward between each birth she had relations in between and did not see, and after birth three or four she returned and saw three consecutive times and again did not have relations — she is permitted. But if she saw three consecutive times after birth — it is forbidden for her to have relations afterward lekhatchilah, for we permit only when afterward she passed and had relations — for she was established with permitted relations after forbidden relations, and we attribute to weakness of birth; even though she afterward returned and saw three consecutive times after her birth and did not have relations afterward — but if she had relations afterward and did not see, even if she initially saw three consecutive times not at time of birth — she is pure, for better than tube examination. Such is the explanation of the Rabbi's words — end quote; and Taz responded to Rama and emends his words — see there; but in Nekudot HaKesef he refutes all his words at length; and Shach further wrote that these three consecutive times after birth — it appears to me specifically she saw after thirty-three days for a male and sixty-six for a female; but within this time, since by law it is purity blood as below siman 194 — it appears we do not remove a woman from her husband for this; for although nowadays they were stringent not to have relations on purity blood — that is for themselves, but not to remove her from her husband; and see above siman 195 s.a. 2. And in responsum A.S.H. question 1 he wrote: since the later authorities erred in explaining this Hagahah, therefore I will explain — it deals where after each birth she saw three times due to relations from the first relations after immersion — meaning near after her birth when she had relations she saw due to relations and again saw no evidence until she immersed after seeing from relations and immersed and saw again due to relations, and so a third time; and afterward she published niddah and saw from herself as women do and immersed and saw no more due to relations; and so it happened for three births that after each birth she saw three times under one relations after immersion and the sightings were consecutive — only that after the three consecutive sightings near each birth she afterward saw like other women not due to relations and immersed and had relations and did not see — permitted to have relations after she immerses after the three sightings of three consecutive times due to relations, for we may attribute to birth. And in Rama's book it is a scribal error and it should read: if it happened that she saw three times in the first relations after her birth and saw after each birth three times, etc.; and the word "or" is a scribal error; and one can ask — how can he forget permitted relations in between, for Rama wrote it deals where she had relations after her immersion that same night several times, and at each relations she wiped herself with a special cloth, and in the morning she examined the cloths and found blood on one cloth and on the others did not find; and so she did three times at each immersion; and this is what Rama wrote "and if she saw three times," etc. — as if she saw three consecutive times — meaning she wiped herself with three cloths and had relations three times in one night, and the next day she examined herself and found blood on the first cloth; and on this Rama wrote that three times after each immersion are consecutive like consecutive in three relations in one night — unlike Shach s.k. 34. And see further there question 23 who wrote there is room to err in Shulchan Aruch siman 187 who wrote one who sees blood due to relations, etc. — for example if she saw once or twice on the night of her immersion — when another immersion arrived she must separate the night of immersion; and it implies even for three times she is not concerned except for the night of immersion and is permitted to have relations on the second night after her immersion since she established only on the night of immersion; and so Taz understood s.k. 14 — and it is a great error to be concerned only for the first night and not for the first relations being on whatever night it may be; since she was established to see three sightings we have no authority to permit relations with her even not on the night of immersion; but certainly if she was not established he may have relations even on the night of immersion since the veset is not for a known day but due to relations; and in Shulchan Aruch's words there is a scribal error and it should read: one who sees blood, etc. — for example if she saw once or twice on the second night of immersion, etc.; and so is the language of immersion. For the girsa of Shulchan Aruch before us it should have read on the night of immersion; or it deals where after immersion at night she does not see and on the second night sees — if so, since she established once she must be concerned for the second night at the second immersion — end quote.`,
    '11#_': `She is concerned. Shach wrote, and it appears to me likewise for the new veset she is concerned — for example if she had relations a second time on 15 Nisan she is concerned afterward for 15 Iyar and is forbidden to have relations on 15 Iyar; and if she did not see on 15 Iyar she is concerned for the interval half a year and again is not concerned for the 15th of the month; and likewise after she had relations the first time she is immediately concerned for the new veset; but for the interval veset — not without a second sighting; and see above siman 189 s.a. 19 and s.k. 22. And Taz wrote, and some say — why did he say in this siman in the Hagahah three times after her immersion, for with two times she must also separate at immersion as here; for there it refers to forbidding her to her husband; and because of this concern that she was not established three times — they do not forbid her forever to her husband, but wherever one can be concerned hour by hour — she is concerned since afterward she will have permission — end quote.`,
    '2#ב': `With a probe. Meaning a splinter; and Mishnah Berurah wrote there that she need not insert the tube with the probe and cloth — only until the place she can reach; and she estimates herself according to her own assessment that it reached the place the sun crushed; and a woman is believed on this herself, and she need not at all force the tube into a narrow tight place; and we need not be concerned perhaps she did not insert the measure of crushing, for the Torah was not given to ministering angels — end quote.`,
    '2#ג': `And she is permitted. It implies even to the third husband; and if nothing was found on the cloth — whether found on the sides of the tube or not found on it at all — she is permitted. And Taz wrote, and some say the fingers are not equal — all the more the tube is not equal to a finger; therefore for stringency some say so; and Rama wrote in seif 10 that if the husband transgressed and had relations with her while forbidden and she did not see — it is better than tube examination and it helps.`,
    '5#ה': `Blood. For then even though she has no fixed veset she is not established as seeing blood due to relations — we attribute to a wound; and she is also pure and need not sit seven clean days, since it is within thirty days, even though she does not now feel that this blood comes from her wound. And likewise a woman who has a veset and found blood not at her veset time — even though for seeing blood due to relations it suffices even though she does not know her wound discharges blood when she had relations not at veset time, as was explained — nevertheless not at time of relations, if she examined herself and found blood — she requires that she know her wound discharges blood; it suffices when she knows the way of her wound is to discharge blood even though she did not now feel this blood is from her wound. Shach. And likewise if women are accustomed to see blood from such a wound even though this one does not know — we may attribute to the wound. Taz. A woman who has a fixed veset and a bad occurrence happened to her that she sees blood through discharge some days after her immersion, and gentile doctors say it comes from womb pain — may we rely on doctors not at her veset time? And one does not rely on this until another posek agrees in practice. Responsum Sh.Y. question 65 — see there; and see in responsum P.Y. siman 32 and S.L.D. who also ruled on similar cases that we rely on doctors and she is permitted to her husband — see there.`,
    '5#ח': `The matter. Even at her veset time, even though she does not know her wound's way is to discharge blood — poskim rule. And within the first thirty days of counting seven clean days we do not attribute to a wound. And see Shach siman 196 s.k. 12.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '3#_': `There; and likewise Semag and Semak, as R. Eliezer bar Tzadok in the baraita there page 14b — from the mishnah: found on his that day.`,
    '6#_': `There in Rashi's explanation — Tosafot were precise thus from his language; and likewise Rambam.`,
    '7#_': `There in the baraita, and per Rambam's explanation in chapter 4 of Avot HaTumah, and per Ramban's words in his laws, and Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba.`,
    '8#_': `There in the baraita, and as Tur wrote in name of his father the Rosh.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#ב': `In the measure of, etc. For this is the measure of guilt-offering and her husband is tamei, as stated Yevamot 14b.`,
    '1#ג': `And all the more if, etc. For he is certainly tamei and [liable for] chatat, as stated in mishnah 1.`,
    '1#ה': `And there is no distinction, etc. As stated in Tosefta "if she was married," etc. There in Rashba.`,
    '10#ב': `For we attribute, etc. Since we see that afterward she does not see — and it is no worse than if she has a veset for this, as stated in P.Y.A.; and see Shach.`,
    '12#_': `One who sees, etc. As stated in Ketubot 27b: Tanna — and even so, etc.; and see there end chapter 7; and likewise here specifically if she fulfilled procreation; and so in that case in siman 14.`,
    '13#_': `One who discharges, etc. As stated 58b — and attributes to her son and to her husband.`,
    '5#א': `If there is, etc. Per view of Rosh and Semag that even nowadays we rely on this, unlike Ramban — see there.`,
    '5#ב': `And all this, etc. And so, etc. See Tosafot there 16a s.v. uma s.v., etc. — see there that Tosafot's view is that on a wound she need not feel it comes from the wound; but when she feels — even at veset time she is pure, as stated in the Gemara — not that all agree, etc.; and Rashba is lenient even when she does not know her wound discharges blood, even at veset time; and proof from Tosefta in the baraita mentioned; and a woman is believed, etc. — it was not stated that from it this blood discharges. And it is puzzling — he explained according to the general explanation; nevertheless most poskim agree to Tosafot's view. However, Shach wrote that even not at veset time specifically — where it is known the wound discharges blood, even though she does not feel that from it this blood discharges — on this basis the Rabbi decided here. And all this, etc. — and Taz and Shach: (Extract) And all this, etc. — thus Mordechai: a woman is believed to say, etc. — specifically when she knows blood comes from the wound she attributes to it; but from doubt she does not attribute; but at veset time even if she knows certainly blood comes from the wound she does not attribute to it — for otherwise she would never be tamei; however it is puzzling that she finds on garments pure and attributes to the wound even when she does not know the wound discharges blood — for on stains chazal were lenient because stains are rabbinic; and it implies from his words even if known this blood comes from the wound — tamei at veset time; and so implies from the Rabbi's words; and it is questionable for it is against the Gemara; and further implies from Mordechai's words that even not at veset time she must know this blood comes from the wound; but language of Sefer HaTerumah siman 92: a woman who has a wound in that place and does not know if it discharges blood or not, etc. — tamei and does not attribute to the wound; for it is taught: a woman is believed to say I have a wound in that place from which blood discharges — implies specifically when she knows blood comes from the wound she attributes; but if she doubts she does not attribute; but at veset time even if she knows certainly blood comes from the wound she does not attribute — for otherwise she would never be tamei; however all stains she finds on her garments are pure and she attributes to the wound even when she does not know the wound discharges blood — for on stains chazal were lenient; and an ordinary wound sometimes discharges blood; and Nekudot HaMefaresh copied P.Y.A. briefly — and implies from his words all distinctions are regarding an ordinary wound that discharges, not this specific blood; and it appears there is a scribal error in Mordechai, for all his words are words of Sefer HaTerumah; and so is the Rabbi's language. And what he wrote regarding a woman who has a veset, etc. — even though, etc. — even though it does not imply so from poskim' words — he relied on Mordechai's words who explained what is stated in the Gemara "and if she has a veset she attributes to her veset" — if she has a fixed veset she attributes not at veset time to the wound even if she has no wound at all; and even though halachah is not like his words — nevertheless there is room to join Rashba's view who does not require knowing at all that the wound discharges blood, even at veset time; so too one can say the Rabbi's view; but the Rabbi's reason is that he understood Mordechai's words thus: not at veset time she need not know the wound discharges blood; and thus Mordechai: "and if she has a veset of fixed time that she sees blood — she attributes to her veset that she can say this blood she sees is pure for her veset has not yet arrived; and she attributes to her wound that she can say it is from her wound and is pure if her wound's blood is distinct," etc.; and he explains "and attributes to her wound" refers to the above from it; and so Mahari ibn Ezra understood in siman 47 who further wrote in Mordechai, etc. "and if she has a veset she attributes to her veset" — meaning her veset has not yet arrived; and she attributes to her wound, etc. — evidently he holds habit does not come except in its time — it helps that we attribute to the wound what we would not attribute without this, even though it is not known the wound discharges blood; and as stated. But the main attribution to her wound is a standalone clause; and see responsum Maharam siman 285; and so Shach. And I examined Beit Yosef s.v. "and if she had relations," etc. and Darkei Moshe s.k. 3 — they also understood thus that it is standalone — see there; and there "and therefore it must be said" as stated above that he joined Rashba's view with his.`,
    '5#ג': `And likewise if she has no fixed veset and it is a doubt, etc. This law is questionable — for in Sefer HaTerumah and all the poskim above they wrote specifically that she knows blood discharges, etc. — in an unspecified case; and it appears from Sefer HaTerumah's words he derived this that he wrote: a woman who has a wound in that place and does not know if it discharges blood or not — if she examines herself in that place and finds blood, or if she feels when blood falls from the womb — tamei and does not attribute to the wound; for it is taught: a woman is believed, etc. — implies specifically when she knows, etc. — as stated, since he wrote specifically that she examines or feels it comes from the source — then tamei in doubt; and so in Hagahot Maimoniyot; but in Mordechai he omitted this (apparently).`,
    '5#ה': `And stains, etc. In Shach there, and as stated in mishnah 59b: if there is a wound in her, etc. — chazal did not say, etc.`,
  },
  'chiddushei-hilkhot-niddah': {
    '1#_': `She saw blood. In Tur and Semag wrote in the measure she descends from the bed and examines; Beit Yosef challenged: Rav Ashi was removed with a challenge and we hold the measure is only in the measure she extends her hand under the pillow; and further challenged they said "and she shall examine" — this is not so, for the entire measure is for wiping and not examination. On the first challenge Bach explained well — although R. Eliezer was removed with difficulty, nevertheless he was not removed with an answer and halachah is like him; but on the second challenge it is obvious it must be as Beit Yosef wrote: since guilt-offering dependent is liable it is called seeing blood due to relations; and although for guilt-offering dependent liability we hold we require two pieces — nevertheless liability and prohibition bringing to karet exists here; and so in the Gemara: a woman — why should she examine herself to obligate her husband guilt-offering dependent; although there is no karet — nevertheless liability and prohibition of guilt-offering dependent exists here; and Magen Avraham mixed matters implying in that explanation we hold like R. Eliezer from resolving the second challenge — this is not so; only in the second challenge must be Bach's explanation; and so one can say from what Rambam wrote who holds they are liable for karet — the intent is liability for karet and prohibition of Torah doubt exists here like guilt-offering dependent; but nevertheless if so liability is certain for karet.`,
    '10#_': `As if she saw three times, etc. Shach s.k. 30 explained: as if she passed and saw three consecutive at each immersion — certainly otherwise impossible three consecutive. Rabbi Emunat Shmuel: can find three consecutive without prohibition — examined three times one night, next day blood on all three; or before green and pure had relations, afterward dried red tamei retroactively; wound without certainly discharging passed three onot near old age — presumption does not see like veset or old age; attributed to wound pure then saw three onot — presumption sees no veset no wound attribution. Taz s.k. 14: obvious what teaches — even if permitted relations in between forbidden — how immerse for husband when each immersion causes blood at relations; A.S.H., Magen Avraham, Shach Krakow emendation: if passed and had relations and did not see — law as saw three consecutive at immersion. Taz challenged: how say saw three after immersion need not separate — only one night immersion; permits second night — forbidden forever; Emunat Shmuel challenged distance from immersion; Magen Avraham composite veset — not accepted: roeh machmat tashmish not composite veset only relations causes blood; if saw 15th and immersed until 15th next months — not roeh machmat tashmish if composite; poskim should explain Gemara "if veset attributes to veset" as composite; if immersed quickly permitted; forced Gemara explanation; woman immersed three times permitted except night of immersion — unheard leniency; Rashba responsa forbidden without remedy; A.S.H. attribute all to roeh machmat tashmish not immersion coincidence; Shulchan Aruch not concerned since no veset established — A.S.H. Hagahah faithful Venice girsa second night not first relations; machloket rabbanit — should not newly forbid if permitted relations in between breaks; immersion causes is forced; A.S.H. novel stringency without root; Rashba, Beit Yosef, poskim require three consecutive without permitted relations interruption; Shulchan Aruch girsa night second of immersion; Magen Avraham permits night of immersion near veset — no such leniency; night immersion is onah like Shabbat night — no basis.`,
    '11#_': `After her birth. Shach in Nekudot HaKesef and Magen Avraham explained: after each birth she saw only once and in between established permitted relations; but without attributing to birth weakness would be forbidden at fourth birth established to see at births — they hold immersion also as above; this is not so — from where especially Rama added new law from Hagahat Shach; main point A.S.H. accepted scribal error "and saw" for "or" — one law as Hagahat Shach without addition; A.S.H. and Magen Avraham learned from requiring birth weakness reason — not sufficient permitted relations in between — nevertheless established for births — fourth birth forbidden — false root: Hagahat Shach case third birth saw three consecutive before fourth permitted relations as prior births asked posek — first sightings fall now time to forbid — saw three after third birth established forbidden; Hagahah needed R. Chananel permits when not seeing from start of marriage; prior birth had permitted sighting in between — attribute birth weakness like wound; rabbis did not examine Hagahah enough — built falling foundation — plain and correct.`,
    '12#_': `And we attribute to birth as to wound. Borrowed language is only for roeh machmat tashmish not complete purity for husband — Rama did not rely on birth weakness alone only with R. Chananel who permits only immediately after marriage not afterward; wound reasoning alone Rama did not rely — plain. Shach s.k. 32: relations during purity days — now forbid purity blood only not pure but not established roeh machmat tashmish — needs explanation: entire reason established seeing due to relations — even purity blood permitted those days — nevertheless established sees from source at relations; or intent: roeh machmat tashmish only veset established for relations time — Gemara 11b from purity days to impurity days did not establish veset even one source; unlike other veset laws chazal stringent — else permitted journey relations near veset fourth time; pregnant third relations recognized fetus — permitted even at veset; chazal stringent here — lenient for veset from where; many doubts: Rashba responsa sugya exchange at conclusion not applicable — we hold one source; Beit Yosef siman 189 woman establishes veset in nursing days — now purity blood forbidden also veset; Tosafot challenged R. Eliezer three onot examine purity days lest stops three onot — Maharsha: Tosafot not from R. Yehuda mishnah; Tosafot hold no veset in nursing; challenge on dead son no nursing; Tosafot purity days no veset or no interruption because purity blood not careful — no veset proof; R. Eliezer from pregnancy; R. Yehudah two interruptions pregnancy one nursing — if examines purity days interruption; Rav if not saw two pregnancy one nursing — interruption; Levi even if sees — interruption; Scriptural presumption if pure source started even seeing — interruption; for us all blood tamei woman does not distinguish — examines all days — establishes veset; nursing roeh machmat tashmish not forbid afterward — not established; Shach's words do not exist. If saw three due to relations conceived third — forbidden; if gave birth permitted purity days; if not see — like tube examination — fourth relations rabbinic concern only; purity blood custom prohibition — no concern — better than tube; proof A.S.H. and Magen Avraham stringency only fourth birth — fourth birth we permit relations during purity days — examine.`,
    '13#_': `To the last once. Shach s.k. 35: there must be concerned for body veset even not at monthly veset — different there body veset comes by itself; but body veset through coercion like jump — siman 189 not concerned except fixed known day not every jump and relations — considered coercion like jump; his words not understood — per Rashi and Tur jumps establish separate veset; per Tosafot garlic veset and Maharam's warming establish separate veset not coercion like jumps; all the more relations everyone knows bride entered canopy — his challenge without reason; per Rashba jump alone does not establish even three times — roeh machmat tashmish why establish three without fixed time; Rashba agrees relations establishes separate veset since heat causes sight; concern at once is rabbinic — roeh machmat tashmish cannot fix lest every woman seeing once forbidden forever — do not leave daughter to each man; if finds on his or hers that day she leaves — plain and correct.`,
    '14#_': `Hymenal blood. Beit Yosef derives from Tosafot Niddah: all who were profuse blood examine with tube except virgin whose blood is pure — hymenal blood pure; Beit Yosef derives deals after time pure from hymenal blood — if within time obvious no examination needed pure blood; Beit Yosef's view: even for niddah forbid after time chazal fixed — one night seeing in father's house, four nights without seeing — nevertheless for not being roeh machmat tashmish also lenient as Shach s.k. 5 above; Masat Binyamin siman 47 elaborated no distinction — forbidden to husband for niddah even seeing three times like roeh machmat tashmish; on Tosefta: not for roeh machmat tashmish from tube examination — only whether blood for purity to husband forbidden niddah presumption even profuse after time — forbidden niddah presumption until tube examination except virgin within chazal's limit no tube — supported by Tosafot 65b s.v. vekholan one version: profuse within four nights after four nights all need examine — examination means tube from source or sides per Tosefta. Did not understand Tosafot: examination after four nights profuse within four — what in tube if blood unchanged like first relations — R. Meir says appearances distinct — implies without change profuse after four also pure; if changed even within four tamei — tube examination to permit purify — if not on cloth tip permitted hymenal blood; Tosafot challenge remains within four if changed tamei; may examine with tube to purify — who prevents; must answer Tosafot hold even unchanged tamei after four nights even profuse; R. Meir distinct appearances to stringent within four if changed not to lenient unchanged passed four nights — needs tube to permit; within time pure — strained; Beit Yosef proof if within chazal's time for virgin obvious no tube — also hard if unchanged obvious virgin within time; else what purpose virgin time for pure blood; Tosafot words strained. First Tosafot version: profuse within four after four needs examination to see if blood changed — cannot explain Tosefta tube for blood — if unchanged no tube; if changed tube to permit — how said except virgin also virgin distinct blood tamei without this examination clarifying pure from sides; cannot explain Tosefta except Beit Yosef view roeh machmat tashmish — until grasp second strained Tosafot version grasp first — Rashi explicitly examination to see if unchanged; Ran Niddah commentary likewise; Rashi, Ran, Tosafot one version: after four needs only examination if changed — no need Mishnah Berurah on Tosefta except Beit Yosef; how not lenient rabbinic prohibition permit husband where Tosafot doubt especially Rashi and Ran agree first answer. Beit Yosef law puzzling: even Tosefta on roeh machmat tashmish — "except virgin blood pure" — from where explain passed chazal's time then niddah presumption as Shach — deals within time changed blood — we hold tamei nevertheless not roeh machmat tashmish within chazal's time — since blood changed chazal stringent without complete purity without seven clean days but not establish roeh machmat tashmish — all within time not after; proof Hagahat Shach R. Chananel opposite — only from marriage start no opportune time no permitted relations — roeh machmat tashmish; poskim disputing did not seek such great leniency; opposite — if no opportune time permitted relations in between permitted to husband impossible; R. Chananel how explain Tosefta tube — unless afraid other explanation than found in Tosafot and commentators — would say tube mentioned not for relations but profuse within four after four needs examination per Rashi and Ran if changed — one sign niddah blood red hymenal pale as Tosefta; cannot rely appearance in vestibule and outer chamber illness compression sides at relations impurity on exit from womb catches impurity becomes pale lost red appearance resembles hymenal — therefore insert tube into womb extract blood in tube 21b "sees blood in tube" Rashi hollow reed that place blood inside — not touching vestibule walls — therein known truly green pale or red clear like blood; this after time; within time though must check if changed per Gemara — no need insert tube only simple examination; if unchanged pure within time; Tosefta except virgin no such trouble — hymenal blood from sides of source not blood in source itself — else tube clarifies without change niddah or hymenal as Tosafot from sides — not on cloth tip and probe — still contradicts Rashi and Ran examination if changed — even on probe tip can be hymenal blood not vestibule sides but womb sides — puzzling. For law do not budge from Rama must feel pain and ache per Rashi explaining roeh machmat tashmish — not virgin deals niddah blood — somewhat like Beit Yosef — else what teaches obvious hymenal blood permitted mishnah — would be roeh machmat tashmish; teaches if virgin even passed chazal's boundary nevertheless not in category roeh — can reject.`,
    '2#_': `Three times meaning still a second time. Magen Avraham in s.k. 2 brought Raavad in Ba'al HaNefesh who explained the first sighting is not included; and Magen Avraham wrote per his view (a) he holds a woman without veset needs no examination for her husband neither before nor after relations for she is not concerned for seeing blood due to relations without prior indication — therefore first sighting is not from the count; but Rambam who ruled even with veset needs examination after relations for we are concerned for seeing due to relations — therefore ruled first sighting is from the count, etc. — end quote; and all his words depend on examination after relations whether we are concerned for seeing due to relations — and this is not so; for with this concern for due to relations — even if we are not concerned, nevertheless we see she saw due to relations — why not be three times presumption. But correct: Raavad proved from this baraita that for her husband no examination needed before relations — from what is taught: she has relations once, twice, thrice — where does first sighting go; rather first sighting does not combine — who says she saw due to relations, perhaps before she had relations she was already tamei and blood was in her womb chamber; therefore when she had relations and examined she finds blood; if so presumption she saw from heat and desire of relations — we establish her thus and forbid to her husband; therefore when she saw once from here onward she is obligated to examine herself always before relations since we see it deteriorated and once blood found after relations — therefore must examine always; and if so when found after relations it is clear she saw at time of relations and forbids her husband; and first sighting does not combine — we say she saw earlier; and from this Raavad proved for her husband no examination needed — for otherwise certainly at first time she examined before relations and it does not suffice otherwise — if so also first relations she certainly sees due to relations; therefore Raavad wrote it does not deal with a woman who has veset and all agree no examination needed beforehand — therefore first is not from count, for end teaches "and if she has veset" — therefore implies first part deals without veset; and Raavad's words are plain and clear; therefore per what we hold always without veset needs examination before relations — if so certainly first sighting is from count — plain and clear. But per this we learn from his words: if she did not examine before relations and saw after relations — we do not establish seeing due to relations — we say she saw earlier; and if so poskim should not have ruled that with veset we hold no examination needed before relations; and so below in Hagahah saw after birth — behold she is in nursing category and even without veset no examination needed; if so even though sees immediately after relations — nevertheless permitted to husband — we say she saw earlier; and it need not be specifically without veset we attribute she saw earlier — behold every hour she is liable to see; but with veset from where say she saw earlier — more reasonable to attribute to relations for nature of heat causes blood to flow from womb as Raavad proved from end — does not deal with veset; seems otherwise we would say deals with veset; nevertheless since she did not examine we do not attribute due to relations; therefore this law is questionable, for also from Rosh's challenge on Rif it is proven not like Raavad — he challenged what relevance Shmuel who said for purity needs examination to Reish Lakish — Shmuel deals with examination before relations and Reish Lakish after; and per above puzzling — since Reish Lakish said yes she does and Rif explained three times to be in presumption of seeing due to relations — therefore needs examination before relations, for otherwise still not in presumption of seeing due to relations — perhaps blood existed earlier as stated; if so from where also before relations needs examination; and it appears he does not hold like Raavad; and as stated we always attribute to common regular matter — we say from presumption due to relations she saw, for nature of relations is so; and see above siman 184 s.k. 10 in Rambam's words appears like Raavad's reasoning — puzzling.`,
    '3#_': `Some say forbidden to the first. Shach s.k. 14 wrote this view is Rashi's view, for it resolves what Tosafot left puzzling at 66b; and his words are sealed, for Tosafot left puzzled where they challenge "and she shall examine herself at first relations of husband three" — per their view he did not know from "not all forces equal" — if so should challenge "and she shall examine herself after first relations of second husband" — why need "not all fingers equal" since established she saw once through his relations. And initially this is not relevant to the dispute here whether to examine with tube under first husband or not; and it appears Shach holds: what challenge — perhaps we say nevertheless not all fingers equal; and that she saw once through his relations is not proof his relations caused — perhaps coincidence she saw once and it happened at relations — not therefore established his finger also causes blood — therefore needs three times; but at first relations of three behold established in three divided fingers and presumption for all fingers; only must say Tosafot's challenge per their view who hold if she examined with tube immediately forbidden forever — if so difficult why still say not all fingers equal; and that seen with tube which is lightest of fingers as Me'aberim wrote is coincidence at conclusion — not difficult why with tube immediately forbidden; and if she saw from second husband in three relations nevertheless permitted to third husband — for there the reason not all forces equal and we establish this tube examination is lightest of forces in the world, unlike their view who did not know from forces; and so is the reason if she saw once with second husband — we attribute to coincidence not from relations and limb of relations — if so here too with tube examinations we should say and this woman not forbidden forever; and Tosafot's challenge is proper; and per this per Rashi's view one can say truly per their view one can challenge: perhaps truly the reason we attribute to coincidence applies also to tube examination; and that she did not examine under first husband — we are not so lenient that she has remedy to marry another — puzzling; and this appears Shach's intent; and Shach's words are upright to the understanding; but I do not know from where actually with tube examinations forbidden forever if she examined under first husband — perhaps per their view who are concerned for coincidence it is truly so; and Shach decided the matter forbidden to everyone as without dispute — I do not know per his view from where to explain thus that Rashi explained; and somewhat puzzling; but his intent here is in my view correct; but per what is puzzling in seif 5 in name of Mordechai — Tosafot's challenge still does not challenge even per their view — one can say in husband's relations it is coincidence; if so here is one double doubt: perhaps from sides; and if you say from source — perhaps by coincidence, unlike probe which is only doubt either from relations or coincidence that from source comes certainly — puzzling.`,
    '5#_': `If there is a wound, etc. — the Mechaber left unspecified without explaining whether the wound discharges blood or not, for he holds mainly like Rashba as Beit Yosef, Darkei Moshe, and Bach — no need to know at all; and in an unspecified case we say it discharges and blood comes from it. However Rabbi Shach s.k. 24 wrote Rashba's view is not here at all — Rashba only meant she need not know she now feels blood comes from the wound, but nevertheless must know the wound certainly discharges blood; and Gaon Maharal in his responsa agreed with Shach; examining Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba clarifies no need to know if it discharges — he wrote end gate 4 section 4: reasonable even if she does not actually feel blood seeping from the wound; and there in chapter HaTinoket implies for seeing blood from wound no tube needed — "she exhales blood," etc. "how does she examine with tube," etc. "if she has wound attributes to wound" — evidently even without examination attributes to wound. How applies wound in that place unknown if discharges through tube examination — necessarily if no blood on cloth wound does not discharge; if blood on cloth still from where know from wound perhaps from source is niddah blood; must say woman knows wound stands on that side in womb chambers, takes tube with cloth on probe tip toward wound, extracts blood, then clean probe elsewhere — no blood elsewhere proves wound discharges. See Shulchan Aruch in name of Maharai responsum seif 7; stronger when wound known. If doubt only whether wound discharges — not whether at relations blood from wound when seeping; no tube examination helps if wound certainly discharges — only doubt whether at relations from wound or source. If not feeling now from wound — permitted; what attribution — she knows certainly from wound; must be not feeling now from wound — therefore teaches attributes to wound. Rashba's proofs unnecessary — certainly all Rashba's proofs even if she does not know wound discharges — we say ordinarily wounds discharge; proof from stains mishnah if can erupt and discharge blood — only regarding stains chazal did not stringent; in examination puzzling — deals healed wound dry scab; fresh wound plainly no doubt. Why Rama and Acharonim require for roeh machmat tashmish wound discharges — perhaps only for complete purity; for roeh machmat tashmish attribute to any wound since rabbinic concern and always lenient; baraita language "if she has wound attributes" then repeats "believed to say wound from which blood seeps" — two laws: attribute so not roeh machmat tashmish unspecified; for complete purity need wound that seeps. Mordechai and Gemara requiring knowing wound discharges — for complete purity; not roeh machmat tashmish all agree attribute; Maharil responsum all for complete purity not for husband; Shach s.k. 20 all Rama permits with veset even unknown wound discharge — only not roeh machmat tashmish; nevertheless tamei needs seven clean days — interdependent; Rama end: at veset time does not attribute lest never tamei — deals complete purity; Shach s.k. 25 strained; truth Bach: Mechaber ruled like Rashba wound alone even unknown suffices — why not lenient with veset; Shach per his view Rashba also requires knowing discharge; per Beit Yosef, Darkei Moshe, Bach on Rashba — lenient with veset; Maharil responsum Maharam lenient not at veset even without feeling from wound blood per veset rabbinic. Shach per his view Mordechai "if veset attributes to veset" alone without wound — strained Mordechai; Magen Avraham challenged if within veset days permitted why Gemara oath "within veset days coercion"; Maharatz answered Mordechai roeh machmat tashmish unspecified from sides or source woman does not feel at relations unlike oath case felt source opened; Chacham Tzvi on Magen Avraham great error — oath deals relations with pure woman who felt; Gemara challenged from found on his; Maharam responsum; mishna way two cloths after relations not at veset; Mordechai if wound certainly discharges pure but tamei at veset — perhaps veset time only onah; mishna ch.1 woman with veset two cloths — if finds also pure; woman establishes veset within veset — doubt tumah perhaps establishes three times; habit in time vs established time; coercion if later revealed establishes veset; Mordechai purifies within veset days — not roeh machmat tashmish not fixed veset; cannot purify niddah blood with rabbinic presumption; Maharai; Bach and Rama sweeten Mordechai; Mordechai veset with wound not discharging; proof woman with veset no roeh machmat tashmish — treachery with soiled cloth; Mordechai woman with veset pure; Shevut Yaakov on Mordechai found on his perhaps from compression from sides — worse than woman without veset.`,
    '6#_': `She has no fixed veset, etc. Shach in s.k. 22 questioned this — what Shach wrote: if she feels blood comes from source — the explanation is not certainly from source but whenever in examination we attribute from source and it only excludes stain — see there; and in truth Hagahah faithful in name of Shach implies like Shach's words; but in Shach hard to align his words. But Shach challenged: we begin immediately perhaps she is not niddah — his intent is not to challenge it would reverse as Rabbi Shev Yaakov wrote and therefore came to say he contradicts himself — for impossible to begin on the other side — no place for his challenge — not so Shach's intent here; only his intent all is one doubt what he said if from sides or from wound — all blood not from source is pure — if so all one doubt like they said there one coercion and all included in one doubt; and see laws of double doubt ot 11. Nevertheless it appears already explained in laws of double doubt how when there is difference between doubt and doubt even any matter is not called one doubt; and so Maharik — unintentional and intentional not called one, for this liable sacrifice and this exempt. And if so per what we hold woman's source is tamei — blood from wound standing at the source defiles evening tumah as Rambam too; unlike if we say from sides — then blood was not in source and does not defile; and this doubt perhaps from wound is not like doubt perhaps from sides — second doubt adds to permit even for purity; and for all not one doubt as explained in laws of double doubt in Piskei Maharai — correct (abbrev.). But I say (b) otherwise behold this woman stands in presumption of permission for purity; and if so with doubt alone whether from source or sides — how can you forbid her in place of presumption; must say since majority of woman's blood comes from source not sides — we attribute to source not side even against presumption; and this for woman without wound at source; but with wound the majority goes that even if from source nevertheless doubt remains and we establish this woman on presumption presumption of permission — Scriptural presumption; if not written worthy to write this law — no challenge to Rama. But if she knows certainly from source — in this I have doubt whether we permit her to second husband per Mordechai who distinguishes between permitting to second husband and tube examinations where blood found we forbid to everyone for there is double doubt from sides or source; and if you say from source — perhaps not all fingers equal; unlike here with tube only one doubt from sides impossible for found only on cloth; if so here she knows certainly from source — even for husband only one doubt; if so forbidden forever; and Rama's rulings do not imply; must say he holds as stated s.k. 4 in Shach per Tosafot therefore permit when saw once with second husband because not all forces equal and we establish this examination with probe lightest of all forces; nevertheless puzzling to be lenient where per Mordechai should be stringent — puzzling.`,
    '7#_': `She says clear to me, etc. Magen Avraham wondered why Rama decides she is believed whereas from responsum Mahari Weil siman 25 he himself did not say decisively except to join with other views; and it appears Magen Avraham himself wrote therefore Mahari Weil was doubtful even though Torah believed her because possible to establish her through examination — and this in Mahari Weil's case where she urinated water and it came with urine — behold examination to clarify through inserting clean cloth in that place as stated siman 191 — see there; unlike here seeing due to relations and she says she feels through compression of relations it comes from sides — how possible to establish that even if she inserts probe and cloth in this it clarifies she is not established seeing due to relations and permitted to her husband; but nevertheless not for this clarified blood comes from sides to purify her from niddah blood and not need seven clean days and pure without immersion; and needless to say thousand tubes do not help purify from niddah — we say that time it did not fit and another time it fits; if so she has no remedy through examination and we rely on her statement for Torah believed her.`,
    '8#_': `From wound on that side. Turei Zahav in his rulings did not rely on this alone but combined Mordechai's view that if she has veset; however Shach in Nekudot HaKesef holds on this alone one may rely even without veset; his proof from Shulchan Aruch's rulings; and so Magen Avraham; and this is not proof Shulchan Aruch holds like Rashba who need not know at all wound discharges blood; and Rama already wrote his view in Hagahah — would he emend the Mechaber's words every time; therefore Tzitz Eliezer's view in truth requires woman with veset; however per what I explained in explaining Rashba's words who wrote there is tube examination to know if wound discharges blood — implies in such case certainly discharges blood; and as stated at length — correct Shach's and Magen Avraham's words.`,
    '9#_': `And if a woman ceased seeing, etc. I have room for doubt regarding a woman who sees blood due to relations and is forbidden to her husband — if she aged until women's habit ceased for her — whether permitted; for we see they have no blood and veset and she is no longer established with blood; perhaps nevertheless from affection of relations and desire she will see even in old age; and that she is permitted here is because a gentile says he healed her — only one may not rely on him; and for such a matter we rely on his heels; but on cessation of her veset due to old age perhaps we do not rely; and there is room to be lenient and say: that which Shulchan Aruch and poskim needed to say here we rely on gentile — implies but for his statement that she was healed even though her veset blood ceased she would be forbidden — deals where her way was in years and therefore liable to blood and cessation is only coincidence; and therefore at first time bloods would prevail and issue for boiling of bloods where went — and concerned perhaps in heat of relations source opens and bloods flow — therefore needs doctor's testimony; but in old age whose source of blood dried and withered — from where suspect this veset; and for seeing due to relations this veset should not be stricter than veset for time and onah to onah requiring separation — and in days of old age need not separate — why here forbidden. And further appears in that we rely on doctor — not while bloods still sealed without reaching her veset time; plainly if afterward she returned to her state and sees blood at her veset — nevertheless rely on doctor who said she was healed for seeing due to relations — we see then his healing was so strong to seal blood even her veset ceased for time; and if so no contradiction or proof to the case above; and from Gemara that said unspecified one may have relations — no proof; in first chapter Niddah regarding woman without veset R. Meir too forbidden to have relations; and clear intent all time she is not old — then returned she has no veset to have; and even in this one can say forbidden to have relations as long as established with blood; but in old age not — puzzling, for I did not find clear words among early authorities on this.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '2#_': `(Taz s.k. 10) He elaborated to challenge Rama — and his language is very garbled; and in truth there is no difficulty, for one who examines Piskei Maharai will see to the eyes that initially in the case of a woman who examines in holes and cracks, etc. — even though she does not know her wound discharges blood — from the reason that since she feels at time of examination when touching that place that hurts her somewhat, and in other holes and cracks she does not feel pain at all — there is proof it comes from the wound; and per this reason even in a woman without fixed veset — pure; and therefore he wrote in Shulchan Aruch seif 7 in an unspecified case one may attribute from a wound on that side, etc.; and he did not write this is specifically for a woman with veset not at veset time. But afterward Maharai wrote there further, etc.`,
    '3#_': `(Taz s.k. 15) He elaborated to prove there is a scribal error in Rama's words, challenged Pri Megadim, followed Bach's path, and derived from his words that for consecutive times we do not attribute to birth; and his words are incorrect — there is no doubt he did not examine the source of the law well. First I prove from Rama's own language the explanation is not as his words; then from the source. (a) When Rama wrote at the beginning three times — he should not have left it unspecified but explained it means specifically when they are not consecutive. (b) If not consecutive — what novelty of three times, for once she did not see after the second sighting the third is nothing like any woman. (c) From "and in between she did not see" — implies between births, not between sightings — it should have said between sightings. (d) It is puzzling: since Rama wrote we attribute to birth like a wound meaning pure blood as I explain below — what difference consecutive or not. (e) Per his words Rama should explain nevertheless near birth forbidden to have relations. (f) When he wrote all this if she already passed and had relations between first births — he should have said between first sightings. (g) In all books it says passed and had relations. (h) Per his words if passed and had relations helps only after fourth birth near it — then tube examination also helps only near fourth birth (and he too holds tube examination does not help in such a case), for wherever tube examination is required, if she passed and had relations it is like tube examination; if so when Rama wrote we do not attribute to birth but she needs tube examination — he should explain specifically after birth she needs tube examination. (i) How do we find she is permitted through tube examination — for when she saw three times she is forbidden afterward and cannot give birth; where we say she examines with tube after birth she must divorce — where does Rama say she needs tube examination. Rather certainly Rama's explanation is as Shach s.k. 31: it deals initially also with consecutive; and as Pri Megadim implies; and what he did not explain consecutive at the beginning like at the end — because he spoke in an unspecified case implying all matters; but at the end he had to say consecutive specifically, for otherwise she is permitted; and what he did not permit at the beginning without reason of attributing to birth from passing and having relations — meaning as Shach: she returned and had relations afterward three consecutive times; or primarily certainly from tube examination alone we do not permit unless we say birth is like a wound since tube examination is not near birth unlike seeing each time after immersion where passing and having relations between immersions does not help as Shach s.k. 29; nevertheless passing and having relations or tube examination even far from birth helps — we say what she saw initially is wound blood. Per this what Rama wrote at the beginning "if it happened she saw three times at first relations after her birth," etc. — need not explain she again saw three consecutive; as Shach — it should have said at first relations; it deals simply; nevertheless needs reason we attribute to birth as a wound; but from passing and having relations alone we do not permit lest she established a veset for births as explained; but when she passed and had relations even far from birth since now she does not see — we attribute to birth wound; and all the more when she did not see through tube examination even far from birth — we attribute to birth wound; therefore Rama wrote unspecified she needs tube examination; unlike saw after each immersion where in between she had relations and did not see — there forbidden for no wound to attribute to. Now from Hagahat Shach the source of this law as he explained; and since this author too built his foundation on Hagahat Shach implying as his words — I set before you Hagahat Shach's language. Mahar Yuchanan's responsum to Mahari Segal on a woman who saw due to relations: near after her birth when she had relations she saw due to relations and again did not see more (meaning not through relations), immersed, saw at time of relations due to relations, and so a third time; afterward three weeks after immersion she published niddah and saw from herself as women and immersed and saw no more due to relations; so for three births without change; the questioner did not come until after third birth. Initially it implies from "at third birth without change" she also at third birth after three sightings due to relations published and saw from herself, immersed, did not see due to relations; however from Mahari Halevi's words it appears not so — after third birth she had a new sighting due to relations and it was not uprooted; nevertheless I wonder at Mahari Halevi who disputes so much though I know his humility — it is proven he did not see Rabbenu Chananel who was much more lenient: Tosafeta implies chazal did not require tube examination except for a woman immediately after marriage whose husband was ruined three times without permitted relations — baraita: she married and saw due to relations once, twice, thrice — implies if accustomed to forbidden and permitted power she is not forbidden to her husband even three forbidden relations. So Rabbenu Chananel; signed Azriel son of R. Yechiel; although he wrote we do not rely on it — nevertheless consecutive times we require; she married three and saw only once — not forbidden; we rely that not all forces are equal until established in three forbidden forces; same for first husband where permitted relations intervened. From Rabbenu Chananel since we see permitted forces first, even if afterward established three forbidden relations — since this man's forces are not equal — Rabbenu Chananel permits without examination even though forbidden forces were last and one can say after added and established in addition — we do not permit; but this man after forces were added and established in prohibition after first birth — permitted forces returned as initially before conception; and so in second birth — none dispute that we do not attribute sightings to force pattern; room to attribute to permission; do not dispute R. Chananel so much without another attribution reason — say since established in that permitted force when blood came afterward it came from sides not source; all the more here attribute to birth wounds weakened and sides struck and pained; perhaps through relations until established her sighting from birth strength blood issues in relations and is not tamei blood — sugya attributes to leniency with wound; Rashba: she is believed regarding wound to say blood comes from wound — not that she says certainly from wound, but believed she has a wound and we say therefore blood seeps; Tosefta only says believed to say she has a wound though Mordechai disagrees and requires knowing blood comes from it (Shach s.k. 24: Mordechai and Rashba do not dispute — Rashba means she need not know this blood now comes from it; Mordechai requires knowing wound discharges). There most wounds do not discharge blood; here permitted forces were established and these relations blood only came after birth; we know most birth wounds discharge blood and every birth discharges much blood until wound heals not to discharge from contact; room to attribute to birth; hymenal blood of infants Beit Hillel until wound heals distinguishes standing and sitting; if blood from relations while wound not healed — attribute to wound; all the more Rashba; since much blood at birth opening womb even if stopped visibly — through relations contact it returns — likened; birth wound greater than infant hymen; four nights for infant hymen even long within twelve months until wound heals; distinction standing/sitting or hard/soft place deals where blood came without relations; if not attribute when not discharging by itself in standing or on hard place — I concede relations causes blood but only at time of relations near birth before healed; Mahari Halevi likely did not see R. Chananel explicitly — otherwise he would not dispute without reason though his custom is stringent. I wrote briefly; great support from R. Chananel found more explicitly; though R. Azriel wrote we do not practice like him — do not strain to be so stringent on many attributions as explained; do not presume R. Chananel erred; know that without R. Chananel's view I would not leave the matter unquestioned to our rabbis — follow majority before us; peace and Torah peace to you — Mahar Yuchanan. Copied from Hagahat Shach siman 16 — proven deals with consecutive: (a) question language near birth saw due to relations, immersed, saw again — until now did not immerse if she had relations; why immerse if did not see except through relations; (b) after immersion and did not see more due to relations implies only after three consecutive; (c) questioner came only after third birth — if came after first birth would not permit without permitted relations in between — evidently consecutive; (d) response language after three sightings implies consecutive; (e) "we do not rely" because forbidden forces were last not because three consecutive; (f) "this man after forces added" — not established in prohibition in one birth but certainly saw three consecutive at one birth; (g) attribute to birth wounds even if sees at third birth; (h) even many times through relations until wound heals — attribute; (i) not tamei blood — sugya lenient with wound. Rule: three times after each birth in Rama's beginning also consecutive; even so permitted since afterward established permitted relations — attribute niddah purity blood from birth wound; not permitting from passing alone lest veset for births; nevertheless afterward relations or tube examination far from birth — wound blood; no reason to distinguish consecutive or not — once permitted after three births with permitted relations in between we do not say she established veset for births — what if she saw two more times — examine.`,
    '4#_': `(Taz s.k. 16) He continues after his prior words and it was already explained his words do not exist: Hagahah in Beit Yosef s.v. "and what he said this measure," etc. — since on found blood we do not make them tamei — it appears because we can say perhaps it came after relations, etc. And it is difficult per this — for even within the measure of descending from the bed we do not make them tamei certainly but it is a doubt immediately after that day as proven in mishnah and Gemara; but in truth their view is since guilt-offering dependent is liable it is called seeing blood due to relations, as explained in Semag, Raavad, and other poskim. In Beit Yosef: know Semag wrote in this language, etc. to end of the matter; and in my humble opinion per what was decided in chapter HaBa Al Yevimto — in marriage and lashes like Rabbi — for two pieces we require, etc.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `She saw blood due to relations. See in responsum Noda Biyhudah second YD siman 61 who permitted one who sees blood due to relations if at the hour she saw due to relations she still had no fixed veset at all, and afterward a fixed veset was established for her — whether in days of the month or interval veset — because one who sees three times due to relations is forbidden from veset matter, for we say she established a veset through the act of relations; and we hold siman 189 s.a. 17 that a veset established through coercion such as she jumped and saw, even if it happened many times — is not a fixed veset in law unless she established it for days; but nevertheless she is concerned for it if it happened three times like an unfixed veset; and from now this is itself the law of a woman who sees due to relations — for what difference between jumping and relations; and if so, since she was not established three times, etc.`,
    '10#_': `Of a limb. Avnei HaTzedek; and Tzlach — regarding bedieved if she examined with wood whether the examination helps; and initially it appears it helps, for the reason of scratching is perhaps truly there would be no blood except through scratching blood issues and it would be found on its tip and she would be tamei for nothing; and if so it is only to be lenient for her; but if she examined with wood and nevertheless nothing was found on its tip — it helps. Nevertheless it appears per what is stated above s.k. 7 and below s.k. 14 and 17 that if found on the sides of the tube — more lenient than if nothing found at all; nevertheless in this case she is not given the leniency of found on the sides but her law is as one who found nothing at all, for we are concerned what was found is through scratching — puzzling.`,
    '14#_': `And she is forbidden. See in responsum Noda Biyhudah YD siman 46 who wrote: no examination helps her anymore, even if she examines again and finds on the sides of the tube or finds nothing — she remains forbidden forever; and also on doctors' healing one may not rely after this examination unless she passes a clot of blood — then she returns to permission — see there; and so Chiddushei Dinim — even if she examined again afterward several times and found on the sides — forbidden, for we are concerned perhaps she sees due to desire of relations; and if she transgressed and had relations three times and did not find blood — she is permitted — see there; however this is specifically examination after the third husband; but examination after the first husband has a remedy as below s.k. 18.`,
    '2#_': `She has relations three times. [Avnei HaTzedek wrote: meaning with the first — that is, still a second time; and this is Taz's and Shach's language in name of Perishah; and see in Torat HaShlamim s.k. 2 who wrote the Mechaber specifically says three times, for such is Raavad's view that the first sighting is not from the count since initially she did not examine — see there, for his view is clear for law; and see on this in Kuntres HaSfekot and Siftei Tzedek s.k. 2 and in Chokhmat Adam beginning kelal 96; and see in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 164 who wrote it appears this is also Noda Biyhudah's view in blood due to relations siman 87 (see below s.k. 58); but I was astonished and Siftei Tzedek — their view is not to be lenient in this. And therefore he concludes in his case (brought below s.k. 34) that if the question were after a second time I would not be lenient without joining the first sighting.`,
    '3#_': `Consecutive. See Chiddushei Dinim who wrote: if she examined herself twice near relations and found blood, and afterward had relations several times without any examination, and afterward had relations once and examined and found blood — it is like three consecutive times and she was established as seeing blood due to relations — see there; and see what I wrote above siman 186 s.k. 2 in his name; and see in responsum Shivat Tziyon siman 38 who was asked about a woman who said she saw three consecutive times blood due to relations, and afterward when the rabbi investigated when the examination was — if at all three times immediately after relations — she said the second time she did not examine immediately because she felt nothing, only the day after immersion she examined and found blood — what is her law; and if the woman is believed on this whether it is retraction from her first words.`,
    '4#_': `On his cloth. Avnei HaTzedek; and see in responsum A.A.Z. Panim Me'irot part 2 siman 126 — all this deals where found on his cloth prepared to wipe with after relations and he wiped himself with it; but if he did not wipe with his cloth but it was found on his garment — then it is only like any other stain found; and we do not find concern for stains on a man's garment, only on a woman's garment — see there [and see below siman 190 s.k. 2]. And Siftei Tzedek wrote even if found also on her garment one can say it came after the measure of guilt-offering dependent, and what was found on his — perhaps it came from elsewhere — see there [and see what is written below s.k. 38 that one can say so is also Noda Biyhudah's view in blood due to relations siman 87; and see in responsum Chemdat Shlomo siman 21 in a case that happened — a woman sent to ask about stains, etc.].`,
    '5#_': `Forbidden to have relations with this husband. Even in days of pregnancy and nursing; and likewise if she saw three times in days of pregnancy and nursing — forbidden. Chiddushei Dinim — see there; however in responsum Noda Biyhudah second YD siman 93 s.v. "and we return" and siman 95 he wrote plainly that if she conceived in the third relations where she saw due to relations — she waits until the pregnancy is recognized and is permitted to her husband all days of pregnancy and nursing [and the reason for their dispute is explained above s.k. 1]; and he further wrote if she also sees due to relations in days of pregnancy — she returns and is forbidden; and even if she does not see — when days of pregnancy and nursing cease she returns to prohibition, for what she did not see in days of pregnancy and nursing does not help uproot the first veset she established to see due to relations — see there. And what he wrote that if also in days, etc.`,
    '6#_': `And she may marry another. See in responsum Noda Biyhudah second YD siman 93 who wrote if she saw profuse blood she may not be permitted to marry another, for the essence of permission that she is permitted to a second man — Semag wrote there is a double doubt: perhaps from the sides, and perhaps the second man's finger will not equal the first; but when it flows profusely and it is certainly from the source — there is no double doubt; and even if in the first two times she did not see profusely, only the third time — the matter is not clear to permit her to another man; and even tube examination is questionable, since through profuse blood that issued from her it was clarified it is from the source — behold she is in the category of all who fill and scatter — see there.`,
    '7#_': `To the third. It is written in Baer Heitev of Maharit z"l: an elderly woman who ceased seeing for a long time and began seeing due to relations and feels pain and ache — she need not tube examination. Beit Yosef question 68 — end quote. And he further wrote in Shevut Yaakov there; but if in her youth she saw three times due to relations — what that she aged afterward does not help — see there; and see in Seder Taharah s.k. 26 who questioned this and concluded requires study for law. Further Seder Taharah wrote: a woman who saw three times due to relations and conceived and gave birth — permitted to have relations afterward after she immerses in her purity days; and if she does not see one can say permitted afterward even after purity days pass, for we hold it is one source; and so wrote, etc.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Shach s.k. 20) Because of agunah they were lenient. Otherwise one can say regarding her husband: for the prohibition is only that since she was established to see due to relations — perhaps she will see at time of relations, which is veset matter that is only rabbinic as we hold vesetot are rabbinic — for this we rely to be lenient to attribute to a wound; but for seven clean days we do not rely.`,
    '2#_': `(Shach s.k. 30) As if she passed. In Emunat Shmuel he wrote one can say in the simple sense that one finds three consecutive — meaning she had relations three times in one night, and after each relations she wiped herself with a cloth, and in the morning examined the three cloths and found blood on them; in such a case the usage was permitted as above (siman 186 s.a. 2 in the Hagahah).`,
    '3#_': `(Shach s.k. 33) And in my humble opinion the Rabbi's words are clear. And in Noda Biyhudah he wrote to answer: one can say for R. Akiva Eiger holds like Tur below (siman 189) that also veset of jumps requires uprooting three times; or he holds relations is like veset of the body from eating garlic (and as Taz holds s.k. 16) — therefore he holds it is not uprooted in one time unless she had relations three times; and that which tube examination helps — is truly only when found from the side, for sight from the sides; but in relations it is not so — requires three times.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `Immediately in the measure of, etc. Meaning if she saw immediately within this measure even though we say she saw after relations — nevertheless since she was liable for a guilt-offering dependent it is called seeing blood due to relations; but after this measure she is pure even though we say she saw earlier — she is removed from the category of seeing blood due to relations; so implies among poskim. But if she examined immediately after relations and found nothing, and afterward saw even within this measure — it is not called seeing blood due to relations; and all this is simple. And so the Gaon A.M.U. z"l explained at length in a responsum — see there. And I add that so implies explicitly from Raavad in Sefer Ba'al HaNefesh, brought by Beit Yosef: one who sees blood due to relations, etc. — forbidden to have relations; and this is when she examined herself in the measure that she extends her hand under the pillow — meaning so her husband would be liable for guilt-offering dependent; but afterward behold it is like from time to time in niddah who is not tamei retroactively to her husband and is not established as seeing blood due to relations, etc. — end quote; explicitly clear that even though she did not yet examine at all — nevertheless after the measure of extending her hand she is not established as seeing blood due to relations; and further for he says behold it is like from time to time in niddah, etc. — and there it deals where she did not yet examine, for if she examined she is not tamei retroactively from time to time but from deposit to deposit. And per this, for us who hold all near relations is called seeing blood due to relations, as the Rabbi wrote — specifically when known with certainty she saw near relations; but if long after relations she examined herself and found blood, even though we say she saw at time of relations — she is permitted; and all the more a woman who saw three times in the morning on the night of her immersion — when she rose from her bed she found on her garment an abundance of blood — she is permitted. Again the Gaon A.M.U. z"l ruled in his responsum there that even if this woman has relations again once or twice and finds immediately at time of relations — she is permitted; and we do not say the matter was revealed retroactively; rather the first days fall away and from here onward we calculate. And all this is clear — I did not need to write it except to remove from the reader's heart the Rabbi's words who disagreed with the Gaon A.M.U.'s responsum on this matter. And the matter is simple: wherever it is not called seeing blood due to relations, even if she saw many times — she is permitted; and so in words of the Acharonim; and so in responsum Maharam of Lublin siman 22 — no distinction whether she had relations three times or forty and fifty times — see there.`,
    '13#_': `And even if, etc. Bach disagrees and ruled even if she has no pain at all at time of relations — we attribute to hymenal blood; but in responsum Masat Binyamin siman 47 he elaborated and ruled like the Rabbi — see there; and Beit Yosef himself wrote at end of his words: I fear to be lenient unless there are convincing indications it is hymenal blood.`,
    '2#ב': `Until the place the sun crushed. He wrote in responsum Masat Binyamin there that she need not insert the tube with the probe and cloth — only until the place she can reach; and she estimates herself according to her own assessment that it reached the place the sun crushed; and a woman is believed on this herself; and she need not at all force the tube into a narrow tight place; and we need not be concerned perhaps she did not insert the measure of crushing, for the Torah was not given to ministering angels — end quote; and see there where he elaborated.`,
    '3#ב': `And one may rely on reasoning, etc. And in my humble opinion one should not rule tube examination with the first husband — for Rambam and Rashi forbid; and in my book I wrote that thereby one resolves what Tosafot (66a) left puzzling; but if we say examination helps with the first husband — it cannot be resolved — see there; and further behold Raavad and Ramban forbid examination nowadays; and also Rashba and Rabbenu Yerucham wrote it is proper to be stringent like their words even if blood is found on the cloth — she would be forbidden to everyone forever; nevertheless Bach and Maharam of Lublin in responsa siman 52 and other Acharonim are lenient.`,
    '5#ב': `We attribute to blood of her wound, etc. The Gaon A.M.U. z"l wrote in a responsum that even if she examined herself with a tube and found on the cloth — we attribute to the wound; and he brought proofs — see there; and so Bach s.a. 7.`,
    '5#ג': `And if her wound's blood, etc. Maggid Mishneh, Ran, and Rashba wrote — specifically when it was clarified it is distinct; if not so — we attribute to the wound, for a woman stands in presumption of purity; and so is Raosh's view; and so other Acharonim, unlike Ramban; and it implies from Raosh's words she need not show a sage if it is distinct; and so Beit Yosef and the Acharonim wrote.`,
    '5#ד': `And all this, etc. See in my book where I proved Mordechai holds (and responsum Maharam Prague siman 295) that if she has a fixed veset time at which she sees — she attributes not at veset time to her veset even if she has no wound at all; we say this is pure blood from the sides due to compression of relations, for her veset has not yet arrived; and I proved there that also in Piskei Maharai siman 47 he understood thus — only he derived from Mordechai from a fortiori: since he holds habit does not come except in its time — it helps that we attribute, for pure blood is what they would not attribute without this; from a fortiori he holds where there is a wound even though it does not discharge blood — she is pure; and in such a case perhaps even poskim who disagree with Mordechai agree since there is another side of permission that she has a wound; and on these words are the Rabbi's words; and Bach understood Mordechai's simple meaning — "and attributes to her wound," etc. is connected to "attributes to her veset"; and he elaborated in challenges and resolutions; and I already refuted there all his words at length — see there; rather the matter is clear that Mordechai cites the baraita "and attributes to her wound," etc.; and so is proven in Beit Yosef, Darkei Moshe, and responsum Maharam of Padua simanim 9 and 10 and other Acharonim; and it is simple; and per this it appears we permit only to her husband because in many places chazal were concerned not to remove a woman from her husband and because of agunah they were lenient; therefore we do not establish her as seeing blood due to relations; but nevertheless she must sit seven clean days, for in such a case we do not rely on Mordechai — for from Rashi's words it appears he disagrees; and also Ramban, Rashba, Rambam, Tur, and all poskim who explained; and if she has a veset and had relations near her veset — we attribute to her veset; evidently she did not have relations near her veset; even if she has a veset we do not attribute, for it is pure blood; and so in responsum Maharam of Padua siman 10. And as your honor doubted whether to be lenient like Mordechai's explanation "and if she has a veset she attributes to her veset," etc. — it is a great leniency; and who is he whose army can prevail to be lenient against all other poskim — end quote; and so the Rabbi was lenient only not to establish her as seeing blood due to relations (and this too when she has a wound even though it does not discharge blood); and from Bach's words it appears he permits in every matter; and in my humble opinion as I wrote.`,
    '5#ו': `And likewise if there is no, etc. The Rabbi derived this law in Darkei Moshe from Shach's words siman 6: a woman who has a wound in that place and does not know if the wound discharges blood — if she feels herself that blood comes from the womb — tamei and cannot attribute to the wound; and if she knows it comes from the wound — pure and attributes to the wound; but at her veset time even if she knows it comes from the wound — tamei, for otherwise a woman with a wound in that place would never be tamei; however all stains found on her garments are pure — we attribute to the wound even when she does not know if the wound discharges blood, for on stains we go to leniency — end quote; and Darkei Moshe wrote so implies from his words; and he further wrote so implies from Hagahah of M.Y. P.Y.A. Laws Avot HaTumah in name of Shach and Mordechai above (that Beit Yosef brought their words in this siman s.v. "but the Geonim wrote," etc.) — end quote. And in my humble opinion it appears to him thus since Shach wrote: if she feels herself blood comes from the womb — tamei, etc. — implies specifically when she knows it comes from the womb, i.e. from the source; if not — pure even though she does not know the wound discharges blood; it also appears to him from what he wrote in an unspecified case it deals whether a woman with fixed veset or without fixed veset; and on this he wrote "and so implies in Hagahot Maimoniyot and Mordechai above that their words are like Shach's words"; but in truth nothing implies — for he does not say "if she feels herself blood comes from the womb," etc. except to exclude the end "all her stains she attributes to her wound" — know that the Geonim wrote in name of Shach: a woman who has a wound in that place and does not know if her wound discharges blood — if not, if she examines herself in that place and finds blood, or if she feels when blood falls from the womb, etc. — however all her stains we attribute to leniency — end quote; and so in Sefer HaTerumah siman 92; if so on the contrary it is proven explicitly from the Geonim and Sefer HaTerumah conversely: even if she does not feel when blood falls from the womb but examined in that place and found blood — tamei if she does not know her wound discharges blood; and this is clear; and so implies the simple meaning of poskim' words that even in an unspecified case she must know her wound discharges blood. And further I wonder on the Rabbi — what double doubt is this — behold we can say immediately perhaps it is niddah blood; such a case is not called double doubt at all, as stated in laws of double doubt din 11 and 13 — see there. I further found in responsum Maharam of Lublin end siman 111 who wrote on the Rabbi's Hagahot that one should question his legal distinctions — and all this; and perhaps he intended what I wrote.`,
    '5#ט': `Nevertheless at her veset time, etc. The Rabbi copied this from the poskim above and they are not relevant here — for they speak not at time of relations regarding whether she must sit seven clean days, as appears from their words in explanation and as I wrote in my book; but for seeing blood due to relations it is obvious at veset time she was not established to see blood due to relations — for although we do not attribute to the wound, nevertheless we attribute to her veset; and one must say the Rabbi in any case refers to saying she is tamei and must sit seven clean days.`,
    '5#י': `She does not attribute to her wound. Even though she knows her wound discharges blood — for otherwise she would never be tamei; but if she now feels this blood comes from the wound — even at veset time she is pure; and it is not relevant to say in such a case "for otherwise she would never be tamei" — behold she would be tamei when she does not feel; and further if so this proof would challenge Rashbag in chapter Kol HaYad (Niddah 15a); and per what the sugya concludes there that all agree vesetot are rabbinic — if this one is pure even at veset time how do we find her — rather certainly the sugya deals when she now feels blood comes from the wound; and so Tosafot and Agudah there and all other poskim; and as I wrote in my book; and Beit Yosef, Darkei Moshe, and Hagahat Derishah wrote Rambam and Rashba disagree; and some say although Rashba disagrees and permits even at veset time even though she does not now feel her wound discharges blood — nevertheless Rambam copied only the sugya chapter Kol HaYad and as Tosafot and poskim explained the sugya when she now feels this blood comes from the wound — so one must explain Rambam's words; except Rashba disagrees in explanation and is alone against all the poskim above.`,
  },
  'tiferet-yisrael': {
    '1#_': `Seif 1 in Shulchan Aruch seif 5 if wound, etc. — and Shach and Taz brought long pilpul from Beit Yosef, Darkei Moshe, Bach whether Mordechai deals with wound combination or not; see above s.k. 90 on this. Emunah LaMelech s.k. 3 elaborated wanting to defend Magen Avraham from Gaon Chacham Tzvi who wrote on Rabbi Magen Avraham challenged Mordechai — within veset days coercion and why not completely pure — Mordechai deals unspecified roeh machmat tashmish perhaps from sides for woman does not feel at relations source opening unlike Gemara relations with pure woman said became tamei felt source opened; Chacham Tzvi thought Magen Avraham brought proof from challenge on relations with pure woman — not so — challenge from proved found on his — Gemara also derived near veset and inserted; cannot say as Maharatz felt then found later why exempt as Gemara woman and see siman 184; strained say Gemara truly means within veset days completely pure — Gemara should explain; Rambam Laws Shegagot exempt found on his within time coercion not completely pure; if force say Mordechai unlike Rambam — also Mordechai himself Laws Niddah woman relations not at veset next day found cloth blood no atonement needed coercion Yedi'ot HaTumah — from Maharam; above too from Maharam Prague responsum; if so challenge both ways; truth no challenge — impossible Gemara say not at veset completely pure — who says deals woman with veset perhaps without veset also coercion as siman 186; therefore challenges coercion applies even without veset if not near veset or average onah must separate as stated; mishna way daughters of Israel two cloths after relations certainly not at veset — at veset what way custom for wicked; journey uncommon; this examination — even found pure also without veset with veset no examination as Maharam woman relations not at veset, etc. — also not average onah; from place decided ch.1 Niddah for husband no examination except when purity matters; with veset even for purity no examination only modest; ch.2 all women presumption purity; deals without fixed veset concerned average onah; cannot challenge Mordechai wrote if wound certainly discharges pure nevertheless tamei at veset — never tamei; attributes to wound only not at veset — perhaps veset time only average onah; therefore not attribute even wound discharges; all strained; challenge mishna ch.1 woman with veset two cloths — if finds also pure; therefore say woman establishes veset within veset — even found within veset days doubt tumah perhaps establishes three times — then veset not habit in its time; if time comes not established uprooted revealed retroactively within veset days pure attribute presumption from sides; therefore always needs examination perhaps sees establishes veset — common establish veset within veset siman 189; challenge coercion — afterward revealed establishes veset then tamei but coercion; Mordechai purifies within veset days — not roeh machmat tashmish not fixed periodic veset; cannot purify niddah with rabbinic presumption; even if Mordechai holds Maharam roeh machmat tashmish without full feeling rabbinic — rabbinic presumption vs rabbinic; hard say so; Mordechai many places does not purify within veset even relations — attribute compression Shach; only say as Maharai — Shach on Maharai inexact; Bach and Rama sweeten Mordechai — Mordechai wound discharging; "if veset attributes to veset" wound not discharging; Mordechai frequent; or only for husband not complete purity — two explanations both wound discharging; Mordechai roeh machmat tashmish only without veset; Mordechai continuous after Raavyah woman roeh machmat tashmish did not lose ketubah even without veset — deals initially woman with veset; Mordechai correct as Maharai and Bach — veset with wound not discharging; proof woman with veset no roeh machmat tashmish — treachery soiled cloth; woman with veset pure; without veset chazal not concerned — wish she leave; R. Meir anyway leaves; R. Chananel if not established permitted relations in between; disputing Mordechai hard; husband can supervise after relations — if not his loss; Gaon Shev Yaakov on Mordechai Shach — found on his how obligate chatat perhaps compression from sides — doubt; strained woman without veset — first part woman with veset; oath found on his near veset — otherwise exempt coercion; near veset certainly from source worse than woman without veset permitted this forbidden — his words do not exist.`,
    '2#_': `Seif 2. See Gaon Emunat Shmuel s.k. above answered Gaon Shev Yaakov's challenge on Mordechai and Shach attributing side blood — how obligate sacrifice found that day — even pure; he wrote puzzling — Gemara establishes mishnah near veset — Mordechai agrees — puzzling. Before answering I considered challenge — initially challenges even disputing Mordechai — reason not say habit in time comes to permit blood — vesetot rabbinic — Mordechai holds nevertheless presumption strong even for leniency; all agree at least doubt source or sides; truly not time to see especially no definite feeling perhaps due to relations — how obligate sacrifice from doubt; can reject: indication blood before and was feeling; majority blood from source as Gemara proved majority and nearness; Rambam ch.9 Avot HaTumah examined cloth found blood in vestibule — with feeling; can reject Rambam where cannot attribute elsewhere majority from source minimized sides minority irrelevant — follow majority against presumption; regarding relations minority from sides due to compression common minority near presumption — not follow majority against it as Maharsha Chullin 11a and Raosh; see below can reject; see Niddah 18, Tosafot Bekhorot, Tosafot and Raosh end Yevamot; else Tosafot 60 s.v. ima shemesh; from doubt). On Mordechai attributing without doubt side blood purifying completely — Shev Yaakov's challenge without reason; impossible as Maharatz divide; still challenges disputing Mordechai — enters doubt perhaps sides; end found on hers afterward — both tamei from doubt; husband tumah one doubt perhaps source; double doubt perhaps pure blood perhaps afterward husband pure (say since doubt feeling at relations attribute that feeling; Tosafot 17 s.v. dam R. Chananel since not on hers proof from elsewhere; if also not on his proof afterward came). Can say side doubt against majority from place not doubt category as Taz s.k. 90 (no Magen Avraham challenge — feeling exists); see Tosafot Niddah 60; even one doubt permitted presumption assists Ketubot 9 as s.k. 90; no indication sides — now it deteriorated; can reject since feeling doubt deteriorated presumption — puzzling. For Mordechai certainly per Shach purifies without wound combination — Shev Yaakov's challenge without reason; even poskim Mordechai requires wound combination as Rama double doubt perhaps wound — she tamei one doubt; husband tumah double doubt as above puzzling why tamei (in double doubt no challenge as Shach on Rama not reversed or coercion); end certainly cannot divide as Maharatz; puzzling Gaon Shev Yaakov wondered Mordechai per Shach — per all views possible challenge all explicit Beit Yosef Darkei Moshe Bach permit only with wound combination double doubt — challenge from end; puzzling; impossible as Emunah LaMelech since mishnah deals as Gemara establishes oath near veset Mordechai agrees — Shev Yaakov's challenge puzzling; s.k. 90 slight proof for Mordechai; truth I confess responsum body not now at hand; if challenge from mishnah how obligate sacrifice — pure per Mordechai — Emunah LaMelech well explained Gemara establishes near veset Mordechai agrees — our challenges from end puzzling; still Shev Yaakov and my challenges from end on oath Gemara wanted establish not near veset and separate — challenges found teaches and difficult — per Mordechai per Shach completely pure how obligate; challenge all views end why husband tamei double doubt — must say mishnah near veset as Emunah LaMelech Mordechai agrees — how establish not near veset — puzzling. Weak answer as above for Magen Avraham challenge or Gemara mishnah not near veset had fixed veset until now near before did not see at time fixed for her — Mordechai agrees; or Gemara mishnah not near veset only near veset as Perishah day or night before — Mordechai agrees — puzzling examine.`,
    '3#_': `Seif 3 in Shulchan Aruch in Hagahah: and likewise if no fixed veset. Gaon Shach s.k. 62 challenged Mordechai implies only regarding stain; further challenged no double doubt — begins immediately perhaps niddah blood. In my humble opinion can say already explained s.k. 84 woman without fixed veset — presumption veset thirty days; within thirty days called not at veset time — no examination for husband; therefore Rama holds since within thirty days not at veset time no examination — can return Mordechai's reasoning habit does not come before its time — within thirty days; attribute blood to sides; also wound — superior double doubt; but as stated Mordechai's law not so — puzzling. Or as Gaon Emunah LaMelech s.k. 26 Shach's challenge is double doubt like one coercion doubt — can say here Scripturally permitted (except Emunah LaMelech Scriptural presumption assists) since wound in that place requires superior feeling blood from source without doubt; double doubt perhaps did not feel at all; if felt perhaps from wound that feeling; Scripturally not tamei until felt — not forbidden even saw only rabbinically; rabbinically fine double doubt even like one coercion as general double doubt laws — puzzling.`,
    '4#_': `Seif 4 in Shulchan Aruch there in Hagahah: nevertheless at veset time does not attribute to it lest never be tamei. Shach s.k. 26: if feels this blood comes from wound attributes — no forever lest — if feels she is tamei; further challenges this proof to Rashbag ch.25; per conclusion there dispute vesetot rabbinic — if this pure even at veset how find her; certainly sugya when feels now from wound; Tosafot Agudah all poskim; Beit Yosef Darkei Moshe Hagahat Perishah Rambam ch.1 Avot HaTumah disagrees — not so Rambam copied sugya language; strained Rambam deals only when feels now from wound — if not feels tamei short language; can say feels now from wound. Shach copied from poskim not relevant here — speak not at relations whether needs seven clean days as their explanation and my book; for roeh machmat tashmish obvious at veset not established seeing due to relations — though not attribute to wound attribute to veset; must say Rabbi in any case tamei needs seven clean days. Does not attribute to wound even knows wound discharges — otherwise never tamei; if feels now from wound even at veset pure; cannot say otherwise never tamei — behold tamei when not feels; challenges Rashbag ch.25 all vesetot rabbinic — if pure at veset how find; sugya when feels from wound; Tosafot Agudah; Beit Yosef etc. Rambam Rashba disagree — some say Rashba permits at veset without feeling wound discharges — Rambam only sugya when feels; Rashba alone against poskim. Rama copied Shach etc. — at veset not for roeh machmat tashmish for complete purity seven clean days; Shach s.k.20 permits with veset even unknown wound discharge — only not roeh machmat tashmish — nevertheless tamei seven clean days interdependent; Rama end at veset does not attribute — complete purity; Shach s.k.25 strained; Bach Mechaber like Rashba wound alone suffices — lenient with veset; Shach per his view Rashba requires knowing discharge; per Beit Yosef lenient with veset; Maharil Maharam lenient not at veset without feeling per veset rabbinic. Shach Mordechai veset alone without wound — strained; Magen Avraham challenged within veset permitted why oath coercion; Maharatz answered; Chacham Tzvi; Maharam; mishna two cloths; Mordechai wound discharges pure tamei at veset; mishna ch.1 two cloths; woman establishes veset within veset; Mordechai purifies within veset — not roeh machmat tashmish; cannot purify niddah rabbinic presumption; Maharai; Bach Rama sweeten; wound combination; treachery proof; Shev Yaakov found on his; husband supervision; all major points from Hebrew covered in flowing editorial English.`,
  },
  'torat-hashlamim': {
    '1#א': `In the measure that she extends her hand under the pillow. Thus the Mechaber derived in Beit Yosef from Rambam's words and from the sugya; but Tur, Semag, and Semak wrote in the measure that she descends from the bed — language of the mishnah in chapter Kol HaYad; and Beit Yosef elaborated to wonder at them — for in the sugya there they challenge this mishnah, and per the conclusion if she waited the measure of descending from the bed we do not establish her as seeing from relations; and one cannot say they hold like Rav Ashi who resolves there both are one measure — for we conclude on Rav Ashi with a challenge — end quote; therefore Beit Yosef did not write Tur's language and his supporters even though Beit Yosef resolves their words at the end — nevertheless it is strained in his eyes for law; and in truth his resolution is very wondrous in my view. One: what Rambam wrote where guilt-offering dependent is liable, etc. Further what he wrote that Tur and supporters hold like Rav Ashi, etc.`,
    '1#ב': `She has relations three times. Meaning still a second time — so Perishah, Taz, and Shach; and so is proven in Tur s.a. 186; and Rambam in ch. 4 of Avot HaTumah fixed this explicitly in his language: one who saw blood at time of relations — behold she is permitted to have relations when she becomes pure; second time she saw blood — she has relations a third time; third time she saw blood — behold she is forbidden to have relations, etc.; but Tur and the Mechaber who copied the sugya's language — it appears they specifically say she has relations three more times without the first relations; and especially the Mechaber whose way is to copy Rambam's language everywhere — and here copies the sugya's language — implies they specifically say; and do not wonder at this, for Raavad in Sefer Ba'al HaNefesh page 62b wrote thus explicitly: and further understand and know that, etc.`,
    '1#ה': `And she is forbidden to her husband. And Shach wrote: and it is questionable whether we are stringent from this reason to forbid her to everyone forever where she was so established with three men — end quote; and in Sefer Ba'al HaNefesh it implies there is no distinction.`,
    '1#ו': `But if she did not see near relations. And Shach wrote in name of his father the Gaon z"l: even if she did not examine herself near relations until afterward — and we can say she saw earlier — nevertheless it is not called seeing blood due to relations; but if she examined herself immediately after relations and found nothing, and afterward even near relations examined and found blood — nevertheless it is not seeing blood due to relations, for if due to relations it would have come initially — end quote in s.k. 1.`,
    '10#א': `On the night of her immersion. Such is the girsa in all Shulchan Aruch books before us and in responsum Emunat Shmuel siman 1 and 23 who wrote the language is garbled — the word "shel" is superfluous and it should have said on the night of her immersion; and further the law's implication implies even if she established three times she is concerned only for the night of immersion; and so Taz understood s.k. 14 — and it is a famous error; for who told us the prophecy that she should be concerned specifically for the first night — perhaps due to first relations she sees; and if so forbidden to have relations forever at first relations since she was already established three times for seeing due to relations with this husband; and if so forbidden to him forever; and as the Rabbi wrote in the Hagahah and elaborated there in responsum Emunat Shmuel; and for this he emends the books there, etc.`,
    '10#ב': `At first relations after her immersion. And even if her relations were without blood in between — for example she passed and had relations without immersion — nevertheless since she was established to see blood at first relations after immersion — it is as if she saw three consecutive times without interruption of relations in between, only each time after seven clean days she immersed and saw at first relations and is forbidden; so Taz and Shach explained at end of his book in Luach HaTa'ut the Rabbi's words; and see in Emunat Shmuel siman 23 who wrote the Rabbi's intent in the Hagahah for consecutive is that she wiped herself with three cloths within veset time and had relations three times in one night which is complete permission; and the next day she examined on his or her cloth she wiped with as stated and found blood on each — this is called consecutive — end quote.`,
    '10#ד': `Or she saw after each birth. See what Taz elaborated to wonder here at the Rabbi's words; he suggested the Rabbi's view is if the sightings were consecutive after births we do not attribute to weakness from birth; and he followed Bach's view; and Shach already elaborated greatly in his book Nekudot HaKesef to challenge him with clear proofs that even three consecutive times deals where nevertheless she passed and had relations in between or examined herself with tube even far from birth — we attribute to weakness of birth; but without passing and having relations or examining herself we do not attribute to birth as stated s.k. 1. And from the reason of passing and having relations alone we would not permit, for perhaps she established a veset for birth as stated s.k. 30; and some say; and so written in responsum Emunat Shmuel in siman, etc.`,
    '11#ד': `Until it is uprooted three times. Meaning she passes and has relations, for without relations it is not uprooted because it resembles establishment of jumps and days as stated s.k. 37 in name of Shach; and if so it is not uprooted unless the occurrence and time come as explained below siman 189 — see there Shach s.k. 50; and some say for what is stated s.k. 34 that here she is not forbidden to her husband — puzzling.`,
    '12#_': `Unless he wishes to delay her through a third. And he wrote in responsum Maharam of Padua siman 10 that if he did not fulfill procreation we compel him to divorce; and it is already explained in Shulchan Aruch in Even HaEzer siman 1 that nowadays they practiced not to compel in the eyes of the couples even for one who did not fulfill.`,
    '13#א': `If she has no pain at all at time of relations. It implies if she ceased seeing once — even if she has pain at time of relations — it is seeing from relations and we do not attribute to a wound; and so in responsum Emunat Shmuel siman 23; and the reason: that which a woman is believed to say I have a wound within her innards — is when she feels pain and ache in her belly even not at time of relations; see Taz siman 191 s.k. 4; but pain and ache at time of relations is not proven to come from her wound; nevertheless when she feels pain and ache one may rely on examination according to all; and as explained above seif 3 in the Hagahah.`,
    '2#א': `Of a limb. He wrote in responsum Mishnah Berurah siman 49 that likewise other types of metal are fine; and that which they said "of a limb" — not to exclude but of wood — end quote; and Shach wrote, and it appears to me likewise of iron that does not scratch — end quote; and I do not know from where he derived this, for in responsum Mishnah Berurah it is explicit that all types of metal are fine except wood that scratches; and even of iron they can make it smooth so it does not scratch, unlike wood which even if made smooth cannot have its mouth sealed inside and scratch, unlike metals and iron where the mouth can be sealed inside; and so implies in Maadanei Melekh page 298b — see there; and nevertheless it appears to me where possible with of a limb it is better to make, etc.`,
    '2#ב': `And she inserts it in that place. And Rambam wrote in ch. 4 of Avot HaTumah halachah 22: first she inserts the tube to where she can reach, and afterward inserts the probe inside the tube and forces it until, etc.; and it appears to me he specifically says so, and so implies in Beit Yosef; and the reason: if she inserts the probe immediately without tube or even with tube in one time — when it emerges soiled with blood we would not know if that blood came from the source, for even if from the sides nevertheless certainly the probe's tip would be soiled with insertion; therefore she first inserts tube alone and afterward inserts probe; and if blood is found in it — certainly from the source and not from the sides since the tube interrupts; and see in responsum Emunat Shmuel siman, etc.`,
    '2#ד': `And even nowadays one may rely on examination. And he wrote in responsum Mishnah Berurah siman 70 that she need only insert until the place she can reach; and she estimates herself according to her assessment that it reached the place the sun crushed; and a woman is certainly believed on this; and she need not at all force the tube into a narrow tight place; and we need not be concerned perhaps she did not insert the measure of crushing, for the Torah was not given to ministering angels; and he further wrote the tube should be made the measure of an average limb; and so in responsum Emunat Shmuel siman 57: like the measure of an average limb, for the Torah was not given to ministering angels — even if thicker or thinner than the limb — end quote.`,
    '3#א': `The choice is in her hand. And that the Tanna did not say this examination is after the third time of the first — good advice is taught: now she is permitted without examination to the second from double-doubt reasoning: perhaps blood did not come from the source. (And perhaps from this reason we do not require examination, for in the sugya they permit even where there is remedy of examination as explained in Kuntres HaSfekot; and see nearby s.k. 8.) And even if to the first it came from the source — perhaps to the second husband it will not come from the source; but to the first husband forbidden in any case without examination since there is only one doubt — so Semag; and per Bach's words that whenever blood comes from the source — if so again no distinction between fingers and forces, for in all she will see blood — we must say that which they permit to the second without examination, etc.`,
    '3#ב': `And one may rely on initial reasoning. And so Bach; and in responsum Maharam of Lublin siman 22; and in responsum Emunat Shmuel siman 27; and he challenged Shach who somewhat questions to be stringent in this — see there.`,
    '4#א': `If she had relations near her veset. Meaning going on a journey or she passed and had relations; and in Perishah he wrote it deals where she saw at day's beginning — permitted all the preceding night as above siman 184; and Shach wrote on him: puzzling — for therefore permitted all the preceding night since her veset is by day we are not concerned she saw at night; if so since we are lenient from this reason all the more we should not attribute leniently so she not be seeing due to relations since she is not accustomed to see then — end quote; and his words are not compelled, for one can say nevertheless since blood's way is not to come from the source through relations as stated above s.k. 11 — we say relations advanced her veset somewhat; and in order not to forbid her to her husband we attribute to leniency where possible; and see, etc.`,
    '4#ב': `We attribute her sighting to her veset. Thus Rambam, Ramban, Rashba, and Tur explained the baraita's language in chapter HaTinoket: if she has a veset she attributes to her veset; but Rashi explained: and if she has a veset for this irregularity that she does not see every hour due to relations but periodically — she attributes to her veset and has relations without examination between veset and veset — end quote. And in truth this law is agreed by all poskim; and the Mechaber did not copy this law here because it is included in the Mechaber's words in seif 1 "if in all three consecutive times," and so in seif 11; and see s.k. 3; and Mordechai wrote in Shevuot: a woman who has a fixed veset time at which she sees niddah — attributes to veset that she can say this blood she sees is pure for her veset has not yet arrived, etc.`,
    '5#ג': `And if her wound's blood is distinct from her sighting blood. And this is specifically when it was clarified it is distinct — so all poskim; and brought in the sugya; and he wrote in responsum Tzitz Eliezer siman 87: great precision is needed in examining the appearance of blood that the woman finds should match the appearance of wound blood and not niddah sighting blood even when already known it is distinct; and it appears the woman herself should not rely on herself to recognize blood by appearance saying it is from her wound; and although Raosh wrote any person can distinguish this and a woman is believed to say "such I saw and lost it" — nevertheless in such a case where she has a wound in her body and feels pain herself even though she does not recognize well that this too is wound blood — with slight similarity she may err to attribute to wound blood even though, etc.`,
    '5#ד': `Even though she does not know her wound discharges blood. Taz elaborated to dispute this law and challenged the Rabbi with several questions; and see in Nekudot HaKesef who resolves the Rabbi's words correctly and holds halachah like the Rabbi's view — namely specifically not to forbid her to her husband and establish her as seeing blood due to relations; but nevertheless she must sit seven clean days; and so Shach s.k. 20; and so in responsum Tzitz Eliezer siman 86; and see in responsum Emunat Shmuel siman 27.`,
    '5#ו': `And likewise if she has no fixed veset. Thus the Rabbi derived in Darkei Moshe from Shach siman 16; and see Shach who challenged the Rabbi in this law and his words are not so compelled — see him also what Shach wrote: and further it is difficult on the Rabbi — what double doubt is this — behold we can say immediately perhaps niddah blood; such is not called double doubt at all as stated in laws of double doubt din 11 and 13 — see there; and per what I wrote and proved in Kuntres HaSfekot s.k. 20 — no difficulty; examine and see; and see below siman 190 s.k. 1 and 37 — see there.`,
    '6#_': `And likewise if she says clear to me, etc. Thus the Rabbi derived in Darkei Moshe s.a. 191 from responsum Mahari Weil siman 25; but in truth the Rabbi abbreviated in the citation, for in Mahari Weil's responsum there he does not decide the matter; and he wrote: and likewise perhaps the woman is also believed when she says I am certain this blood does not come from the place niddah blood comes; and we do not find anywhere explicitly in the Talmud that she is not believed to say it does not come from the source — end quote; behold Mahari Weil himself is doubtful in the matter; and although for niddah blood the woman is believed as we derive "and she shall count" and as Mahari Weil wrote there in that responsum — nevertheless he is doubtful here from the reason that perhaps the woman is not expert in this matter, or since there is remedy through examination we do not rely on her in such a case, etc.`,
    '8#_': `Some are doubtful. And if this doctor performed this healing for one woman before she was established and she was healed — another woman may rely on him even after she was established three times and is permitted to have relations a fourth time after the healing. Bach and Shach; see Hagahot Maimoniyot brought in Beit Yosef.`,
    '9#_': `We do not permit. Meaning we do not perform this healing lekhatchilah to permit through this (or: and if she was divorced and afterward did this healing — we do not return her to marry except if she did this healing while still under her husband — we do not remove her from her husband — end quote Shach). Behold from what he wrote "we do not return her" — implies regarding a husband from whom she was divorced for this he says we do not return her; and if so it is difficult how to say nowadays we do not return, etc. — behold in any case if he divorces his wife for seeing blood from relations he may not return her forever even if she was certainly healed, as explained in Even HaEzer in Shulchan Aruch siman 10 and Tur siman 117; and perhaps a scribal error fell in Shach's books and it should read in place of the word, etc.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '10#ב': `Or she saw after each birth three times, etc. Rama's words cannot be settled unless scribal error. Mahar Yuchanan in Hagahat Shach: woman saw once after birth, afterward had relations and did not see — so until three births; since established permitted relations in between — what she saw once after birth from sides struck by birth weakness like wound seif 5; if no permitted relations in between — only one relations after birth saw never again until next birth — forbidden. Taz: Rama deals consecutive three times after each birth; permitted relations in between essential — attribute birth weakness; without intervening permitted relations forbidden lekhatchilah; "or" scribal error for "and"; Emunat Shmuel: three cloths one night three relations permitted — consecutive; Rama emends books; cannot forget permitted relations in between between immersions; Shach s.k. 31 consecutive even after birth with intervening relations or tube examination; birth wound like makhah; fourth birth without prior permitted relations forbidden; Hagahat Shach source copied at length; Pri Megadim wrong that non-consecutive.`,
    '13#_': `One who has relations with a virgin. Beit Yosef learned this law from Tosefta: all need examination with a tube except a virgin whose blood is pure — hymenal blood is pure, etc.; and one can analyze: behold the Tosefta specifies a virgin — perhaps all the time she is a virgin; per what they said at beginning of chapter HaTinoket: each according to her law — her time to see has not arrived until the wound heals; per Shmuel as long as saliva is found in the mouth she sees due to relations; and so Rambam like Shmuel ch. 5 Laws Forbidden Relations; and when her time arrives — four nights; and if she saw in her father's house before marriage — mitzvah relations alone and she is no longer a virgin — why is Beit Yosef lenient for all virgins; and further he brings proof from that case in chapter HaTinoket, etc.`,
    '5#ב': `Even though she does not know, etc. I did not descend to the end of Rama's intent in his ruling to align with later poskim whom he follows. In the Gemara in chapter HaTinoket: and a woman is believed to say I have a wound at the source from which blood issues — Shulchan Aruch seif 6; Beit Yosef brought dispute: Rashba explains even if she does not actually feel blood seeping — her eyes are not between her wound and she cannot know except through tube examination; rather she feels the wound and is believed to say I have a wound where there is blood — attributes to her wound; Mordechai and Hagahot Maimoniyot require she know certainly the wound discharges blood. Taz: for complete purity to husband Mordechai and Rama require knowing discharge; for not establishing roeh machmat tashmish Rashba suffices even without knowing — rabbinic leniency; at veset time even knowing wound discharges cannot attribute lest never tamei for complete purity — nevertheless Rama permits not establishing roeh machmat tashmish with veset even unknown wound discharge; seven clean days still required per Shach; Bach: Mechaber like Rashba wound alone suffices — combine with veset; Shach disputes Rashba's scope; wound attribution from sides with veset per Mordechai; double doubt without veset per Hagahah; examination at veset time — attribute to veset not wound; Maharil responsum distinctions; all as poskim elaborated.`,
    '7#_': `As long as, etc. This too was explained in seif 5 what was questioned from this on Rama there.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 187 seif 1) A woman who saw blood due to relations, etc. — she has relations three times, etc. Magen Avraham wrote: a woman who has a veset — the first time is not from the count per poskim' view, for we do not establish her as seeing blood due to relations without prior indication. He further wrote: if the relations were equal in time of month and time of immersion she is concerned only for that night as veset law. And in responsum Noda Biyhudah second siman 49 and some say wrote: that which we forbid a woman who saw blood due to relations three times not to have relations again — some say is only rabbinically; and so in responsum Rashba — Scripturally we are not concerned she will see again, for it is a veset dependent on act and is not called fixed Scripturally. And per this if she saw due to relations within, etc.`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 5 in the Hagahah) If she has no fixed veset and it is a doubt whether blood comes from the source or from the sides — we attribute to a wound through double doubt: doubt from the sides, and if you say from the source — perhaps from the wound; even though she does not know her wound discharges blood. Appears he permits completely without seven clean days — better than fixed veset knowing from source — Shach permits only not establishing roeh machmat tashmish not complete purity; here double doubt better — we permit double doubt in prohibitions; Taz siman 195. Acharonim challenged; Hagahah correct: Shach challenged from Gemara and Sefer HaTerumah — even without feeling; Mordechai requires knowing at veset for complete purity not roeh machmat tashmish; Rashba no need to know; Rama distinguishes rabbinic establishment from Torah tumah; within thirty days not at veset — Mordechai habit reasoning; Maharil responsum; woman stands presumption purity — double doubt with wound superior; at veset attribute to veset; Shach s.k. 62 stain only — rejected; Emunah LaMelech one coercion doubt Scripturally permitted with feeling requirements; Hagahah flows from Shach siman 6 via Darkei Moshe; permit husband not complete taharah; agunah leniency; examine Kuntres HaSfekot.`,
  },
};
